WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.820
Good morning Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.380
So I am a total sucker for airport reunions,
and because I've spent about a third of my

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.440
adult life in airports, I've seen a lot of
them over the years.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.280
But a couple weeks ago, I got to see…

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.179
Well, okay, so this is Saw Kyar Aye, waiting
at the airport for his wife and son.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:19.779
He was born in Burma, also known as Myanmar,
and as a young man he was taken from his community

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.860
by the army and forced to work on road construction.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.140
The conditions for forced labor in Burma are
horrible, and it remains a problem despite

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.230
the steps toward democracy the country has
made in the past decade.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.850
Beatings by soldiers are common, and deserters
have been executed.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:38.230
Persecution of religious and ethnic minorities
is also common, and Saw Kyar Aye is both—he

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.210
is a Christian and a member of the ethnic
Chin minority.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:45.560
Saw Kyar Aye escaped his forced labor crew
in 2008 and eventually found his way to Malaysia,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.300
where he registered as a refugee with UNHCR,
but because he couldn’t legally work in

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.460
Malaysia, he had to live on the margins of
the social order.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.899
Then again, he couldn’t risk returning home,
because now he would be considered a deserter.

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:01.390
He did consider himself lucky, though, to
find unofficial work in a restaurant, where

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.460
he met Thet Thet, a fellow Burmese refugee
who was a waitress in that restaurant.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.560
They fell in love and got married, but their
refugee cases were being processed separately.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.369
The bureaucracy faced by refugees is often
quite dizzying.

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:18.630
There are so many layers of vetting and checking,
and only a tiny fraction of people are ever

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.140
approved for resettlement.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.939
Also, critically, if you do get approved for
resettlement, that news can come at any time,

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:28.280
and when it does, you have once chance to
take it or leave it.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.180
So in 2012, four years after escaping to Malaysia,
Saw Kyar Aye received permission to come to

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.380
the United States, but because Thet Thet's
application was being vetted separately, she did not.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.160
Together they decided that Aye should go,
partly because they'd been told it was easy

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.320
to reunite spouses once one was in the United
States.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.649
So in December of 2012, with Thet Thet eight
months pregnant, Saw Kyar Aye left Malaysia

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:51.850
for Indianapolis.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.920
There are more than 13,000 Burmese refugees
now living here in Indianapolis.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.880
Saw Kyar Aye joined that community, but he
also joined the broader Indianapolis community.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.460
He found a church and friends and a job—he
works in a Clif bar factory—but the very

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.670
first thing he did upon arriving was file
for family reunification.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:13.720
Quick pause to note that refugee resettlement
has not increased U.S. crime rates in Indianapolis or elsewhere.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.020
In fact, it is associated with community crime
rates dropping.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:22.180
And also, there is little evidence that refugee
resettlement negatively affects employment or wages.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.640
Sources in the video info below.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.580
Right, so, it's the beginning of 2013.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.750
Thet Thet is in Malaysia, caring for her baby
son, Saw Kyar Min, working when she can.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.890
The family seems certain to be reunited soon,
but delays keep happening until finally, in

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:40.060
April of 2014, Saw Kyar Aye learns that his
wife and son will be coming to the United States.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.660
Except there is a clerical error.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.470
It turns out that there are two Saw Kyar Ayes in
a refugee database, and Thet Thet has been

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:52.740
listed as being married to the other one,
so the trip is canceled, and the process starts again.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.400
At last, in 2016, Thet Thet and Saw Kyar Min
are again approved for resettlement.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.540
Visas are issued.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.060
Travel plans made.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.260
And then comes an executive order halting
all refugee resettlement into the United States

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.070
for at least 120 days.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.700
Saw Kyar Aye said he started to wonder if
he would ever get a chance to see his son.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.940
He'd prepared everything for the kid to start
school in Indiana, but now they would have

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.240
to wait again.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.640
Would the refugee resettlement program ever
restart?

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.510
If so, would people already approved still
be at the front of the line, or would it be

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.050
2014 all over again?

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.091
But then, as suddenly as the executive order
arrived, a court ruled much of it invalid,

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:32.940
and days later, Thet Thet and Saw Kyar Min
were on a plane, first to New York and then to Indianapolis.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.640
Saw Kyar Aye and I have both worked with a
local refugee resettlement organization called

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.190
Exodus, and through them, I was invited to
the airport for the reunion, and walking in,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.070
I was so nervous.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:44.680
Would there be another executive order?

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.160
A different court reaching a different ruling?

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.060
No. This time, I got to witness the best airport
reunion ever, as Saw Kyar Aye saw his wife

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:56.230
for the first time in over four years, and
held his son for the first time ever.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:00.450
No one can seriously think that reuniting
this family poses a threat to the strength

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:05.140
or the prosperity of the United States, but
to be a refugee is to have much of your life

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.070
defined by powers far beyond your control.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:13.090
Yesterday, a new blanket ban on refugee resettlement
was announced for at least 120 days, meaning

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:18.410
that many families who'd been praying for,
and told to expect, reunions like this one

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.410
will now have to wait.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:23.850
Now, we can have—and we need to have—a discussion
about how many refugees should be resettled

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:28.760
in the United States each year, but a blanket
ban is a terrible way to start that conversation.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:34.040
Instead, I believe that conversation must
start by seeing refugees as people with individual

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.090
stories, who love their families and want
them safe.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.080
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.180
P. S. Over at youtube.com/100days, my best friend
Chris and I are on a health and fitness journey,

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:46.010
and on day 100, we’re gonna run a 10K race.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.190
You can sponsor us!

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:51.690
All the proceeds will go to Exodus, the refugee
resettlement organization here in Indianapolis.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.160
Link in the dooblydoo below.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.040
Thanks, bye!

